      "text": "To find the area of the parallelogram generated by the vectors \\(\\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we can use the magnitude of the cross product of these two vectors. The area \\(A\\) of the parallelogram is given by:\n\n\\[ A = \\| \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} \\| \\]\n\nFirst, we need to compute the cross product \\(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}\\). The cross product of two vectors \\(\\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} u_1 \\\\ u_2 \\\\ u_3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\\ v_2 \\\\ v_3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is given by:\n\n\\[ \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ u_1 & u_2 & u_3 \\\\ v_1 & v_2 & v_3 \\end{vmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} u_2 v_3 - u_3 v_2 \\\\ u_3 v_1 - u_1 v_3 \\\\ u_1 v_2 - u_2 v_1 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nFor our vectors \\(\\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\), the cross product is:\n\n\\[ \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} (1)(4) - (-2)(-3) \\\\ (-2)(1) - (3)(4) \\\\ (3)(-3) - (1)(1) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 - 6 \\\\ -2 - 12 \\\\ -9 - 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -10 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of the cross product vector:\n\n\\[ \\| \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-14)^2 + (-10)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 196 + 100} = \\sqrt{300} = 10\\sqrt{3} \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{10\\sqrt{3}} \\]",
